module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_job_waits_while_holding_the_runner_its_dependency_needs

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, MechanicallyPreventable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data a_job_waits_while_holding_the_runner_its_dependency_needs: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_job_waits_while_holding_the_runner_its_dependency_needs" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "two rungs, in order. To MechanicallyPreventable for the whole class: (a) the capability of reading, for every job that waits on another run, the runner labels that run's jobs require, so generation refuses a wait placed on any runner the awaited run can need (the grant's wait is already refused this way), AND (b) an approval transport for the gcp-iam request that is reachable off srv1 over the API, so the iam await can sit on a GitHub-hosted job like the grant await does -- until (b) exists, the iam path keeps the class at Mitigatable by the minimum-across-paths rule. To StructurallyGuaranteed: the wait's placement checked against the awaited run's runners by construction, not per-rule." } },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a job holds a runner slot while it waits for another run to finish, and that other run has a job that needs the same runner. With one slot online, neither can progress until a timeout.",

    "HARM, 2026-10-23T03:5xZ: fleet-converge boot run 39895480515's mtcollins1-boot job, pinned to srv1, held srv1-01 (the only srv1 runner then online) in its step 'Run-scoped grant: wait for the grant run'. The grant run 39895564897's gcp-iam-converge job requires label srv1 and queued behind it. The boot job's timeout was 199 minutes. Both runs were cancelled by hand.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the wait was a STEP inside the job that then used the grant, so placing the job on its host placed the wait there too. The shared fleet-converge job had the same shape for every run-scoped mode, and a dispatch naming host=srv1 could deadlock the same way. The wait itself needs no fleet runner: it reads runs over the API with a read-only token.",

    "SIGHTING, 2026-11-21T08:35Z onward: gcp_iam_converge dispatch run 44885624911 (executor srv1). Its gcp-iam-converge job FILES AND POLLS the operator-approval wait as a STEP (step timeout 259 minutes), so the job holds an srv1-01 slot for the whole approval window. A later fleet-converge dispatch needing an srv1 executor -- swift-tern-46's remote_build_serving_converge apply for srv4 -- queued behind it for over 20 minutes (reported by clever-raven-401, 2026-11-21). The mitigation above does NOT reach this instance: fleet_converge_grant_wait_placement_refusal keys on the branch run grant's await step id only, and the gcp-iam request-wait lives inside the srv1 job by construction (the approval client files and polls over srv1's loopback, so the wait cannot simply be relocated to a hosted job -- the approval read must first become reachable off srv1, the way the grant wait reads runs over the API with a read-only token).",

    "PROPOSED SHAPE for this instance, same class: a GitHub-hosted await job (as fleet_converge_branch_run_grant_await_job) files/polls the iam approval over an API-reachable transport, and the srv1 job's apply step admits only its approved output; the placement refusal then extends to any request-wait step, not just the grant's. Until that transport exists, the instance is OPEN and recorded here, not fixed.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable (job timeouts bounded the hang; an operator cancelled). CURRENT: mitigatable -- the class's rung is the MINIMUM across its in-scope paths (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder, meta-obligation 1), and the 2026-11-21 gcp-iam instance sits only at mitigatable (step timeout, operator cancel). The GRANT-WAIT PATH alone is mechanically preventable: its wait is its own GitHub-hosted job (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow fleet_converge_branch_run_grant_await_job) that the boot and shared jobs need, and emission refuses a workflow in which a job holding that wait has a runner that is not GitHub-hosted (fleet_converge_grant_wait_placement_refusal); test.claim.fleet_converge_run_scoped_coverage_witness the_grant_wait_holds_no_fleet_runner_and_a_wait_on_srv1_refuses is that path's RED and positive control. The IAM path returns the class to mechanically preventable when the standing row's trigger (a)+(b) land. CEILING: structurally guaranteed, once a wait's placement is checked against the runners of the run it awaits rather than against one rule for one wait.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow", decl_name: "fleet_converge_branch_run_grant_await_job", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow", decl_name: "fleet_converge_grant_wait_placement_refusal", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.fleet_converge_run_scoped_coverage_witness", decl_name: "the_grant_wait_holds_no_fleet_runner_and_a_wait_on_srv1_refuses", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
